1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,022
A drop of oil on water.

2
00:00:02,022 --> 00:00:03,032
A soap bubble.

3
00:00:03,032 --> 00:00:05,042
Salt sprinkled on cucumber slices.

4
00:00:05,442 --> 00:00:06,932
The oil never mixes.

5
00:00:06,932 --> 00:00:08,721
The bubble holds together.

6
00:00:08,721 --> 00:00:13,200
And the cucumber slices slowly get
wet, as water comes out of them.

7
00:00:13,650 --> 00:00:15,720
Diya noticed all three in the kitchen.

8
00:00:15,720 --> 00:00:18,959
She asked: what is the thin
skin around every cell made of?

9
00:00:18,959 --> 00:00:21,416
And how does it decide
what gets in and out?

10
00:00:21,766 --> 00:00:22,666
Two answers.

11
00:00:22,651 --> 00:00:26,931
The skin is the cell membrane, made
mainly of lipids, which are fats and

12
00:00:26,931 --> 00:00:28,282
oils, and proteins.

13
00:00:28,282 --> 00:00:30,244
And it is selectively permeable.

14
00:00:30,244 --> 00:00:32,964
It lets some things
pass, and stops others.

15
00:00:33,464 --> 00:00:38,038
By the end, you will know how the
membrane is built, the fluid mosaic model,

16
00:00:38,038 --> 00:00:41,063
and how things cross it,
with and without energy.

17
00:00:41,713 --> 00:00:43,828
First, how did scientists find out?

18
00:00:44,178 --> 00:00:48,176
The membrane is too thin to see
clearly with a light microscope.

19
00:00:48,176 --> 00:00:52,548
Its detailed structure was studied only
after the electron microscope came,

20
00:00:52,548 --> 00:00:54,168
in the nineteen fifties.

21
00:00:54,618 --> 00:00:58,952
Chemical tests on human red blood
cells showed what it is made of: mainly

22
00:00:58,952 --> 00:01:00,287
lipids and proteins.

23
00:01:00,287 --> 00:01:04,668
In the red blood cell membrane, about
fifty two per cent is protein, and

24
00:01:04,668 --> 00:01:06,639
about forty per cent is lipid.

25
00:01:07,289 --> 00:01:09,171
Let's zoom into the membrane.

26
00:01:09,521 --> 00:01:10,997
Here is a whole cell.

27
00:01:10,997 --> 00:01:13,757
The membrane is the
thin line all around it.

28
00:01:14,257 --> 00:01:15,378
Zoom into the edge.

29
00:01:15,378 --> 00:01:18,219
The membrane is two layers
of lipids, a lipid bilayer.

30
00:01:18,219 --> 00:01:19,971
The main lipids are phospholipids.

31
00:01:20,471 --> 00:01:22,873
Zoom again, into one phospholipid.

32
00:01:22,873 --> 00:01:27,668
It has a polar head, which loves
water, and two tails, which keep away

33
00:01:27,668 --> 00:01:28,608
from water.

34
00:01:28,608 --> 00:01:31,900
The tails are hydrophobic,
meaning water fearing.

35
00:01:32,400 --> 00:01:36,869
So in the bilayer, the heads face the
water on both sides, and the tails

36
00:01:36,869 --> 00:01:39,287
hide in the middle, safe from water.

37
00:01:39,287 --> 00:01:42,824
Just like the oil drop, the
tails keep away from water.

38
00:01:43,474 --> 00:01:46,746
Now let's build the full
membrane, step by step.

39
00:01:47,096 --> 00:01:47,996
Step one.

40
00:01:47,762 --> 00:01:50,595
Two layers of phospholipids,
heads out and tails in.

41
00:01:51,345 --> 00:01:52,245
Step two.

42
00:01:52,165 --> 00:01:55,643
Add cholesterol, which also
sits inside the membrane.

43
00:01:56,393 --> 00:01:57,293
Step three.

44
00:01:57,171 --> 00:01:58,466
Add integral proteins.

45
00:01:58,466 --> 00:02:01,291
These are partly or totally
buried in the membrane.

46
00:02:02,041 --> 00:02:02,941
Step four.

47
00:02:02,773 --> 00:02:04,143
Add peripheral proteins.

48
00:02:04,143 --> 00:02:06,480
These lie on the surface of the membrane.

49
00:02:07,230 --> 00:02:08,130
Step five.

50
00:02:07,902 --> 00:02:08,883
Add carbohydrates.

51
00:02:08,883 --> 00:02:12,414
The membrane has proteins and carbohydrates
too, and the amount of protein

52
00:02:12,414 --> 00:02:14,878
and lipid changes from
one cell type to another.

53
00:02:15,628 --> 00:02:16,649
Remember this.

54
00:02:16,649 --> 00:02:19,187
Fluid, because the parts move sideways.

55
00:02:19,187 --> 00:02:21,820
Mosaic, because many pieces fit together.

56
00:02:22,420 --> 00:02:25,166
This picture is called
the fluid mosaic model.

57
00:02:25,166 --> 00:02:28,148
It was proposed by Singer
and Nicolson, in 1972.

58
00:02:28,498 --> 00:02:30,784
The lipids behave almost like a liquid.

59
00:02:30,784 --> 00:02:34,728
So the proteins can move sideways
within the bilayer, like boats drifting

60
00:02:34,728 --> 00:02:35,628
on a pond.

61
00:02:35,539 --> 00:02:38,227
This ability to move is
the membrane's fluidity.

62
00:02:38,677 --> 00:02:40,213
Fluidity is important.

63
00:02:40,213 --> 00:02:45,163
It helps in cell growth, in forming
joins between cells, and in secretion.

64
00:02:45,613 --> 00:02:50,159
It also helps in endocytosis, when
the cell takes things in by folding

65
00:02:50,159 --> 00:02:52,684
its membrane, and in cell division.

66
00:02:53,334 --> 00:02:55,722
The membrane's most
important job is transport.

67
00:02:55,722 --> 00:02:57,202
Moving molecules across it.

68
00:02:57,552 --> 00:02:59,867
Passive transport needs no energy.

69
00:02:59,867 --> 00:03:04,342
Neutral molecules move by simple
diffusion, from higher concentration to

70
00:03:04,342 --> 00:03:06,822
lower, like a crowd spreading out.

71
00:03:07,272 --> 00:03:12,025
Water also moves from higher to lower
concentration across the membrane.

72
00:03:12,025 --> 00:03:13,753
This is called osmosis.

73
00:03:14,203 --> 00:03:17,845
Polar molecules cannot pass through
the oily middle of the bilayer.

74
00:03:17,845 --> 00:03:21,410
They need a carrier protein in
the membrane to help them across.

75
00:03:22,060 --> 00:03:26,383
Some molecules must go the other way,
from lower to higher concentration.

76
00:03:26,383 --> 00:03:27,738
Against the gradient.

77
00:03:28,088 --> 00:03:31,558
This needs energy, from ATP,
the cell's energy currency.

78
00:03:31,558 --> 00:03:33,459
It is called active transport.

79
00:03:33,909 --> 00:03:36,527
An example is the sodium potassium pump.

80
00:03:36,527 --> 00:03:40,359
It pumps sodium ions out and
potassium ions in, using ATP.

81
00:03:40,959 --> 00:03:41,859
Your turn.

82
00:03:41,847 --> 00:03:45,879
Water moves into a cell, from
higher concentration to lower.

83
00:03:45,879 --> 00:03:48,491
Is this active transport, or osmosis?

84
00:03:48,491 --> 00:03:49,798
Pause and decide.

85
00:03:52,998 --> 00:03:53,898
Osmosis!

86
00:03:53,724 --> 00:03:57,548
Water moving from higher to
lower concentration is passive.

87
00:03:57,548 --> 00:03:58,867
It needs no ATP.

88
00:03:59,517 --> 00:04:01,083
Now, a worked example.

89
00:04:01,083 --> 00:04:02,845
Name the type of transport.

90
00:04:03,195 --> 00:04:04,095
Case one.

91
00:04:04,035 --> 00:04:08,976
Water moves out of the cucumber slices,
from higher to lower concentration.

92
00:04:08,976 --> 00:04:10,862
That is osmosis, passive.

93
00:04:11,312 --> 00:04:12,212
Case two.

94
00:04:12,057 --> 00:04:16,357
A polar molecule crosses the membrane,
from higher to lower, with the help

95
00:04:16,357 --> 00:04:17,758
of a carrier protein.

96
00:04:17,758 --> 00:04:19,112
It is still passive.

97
00:04:19,562 --> 00:04:20,462
Case three.

98
00:04:20,454 --> 00:04:25,174
Sodium ions are pumped out, from lower
to higher concentration, using ATP.

99
00:04:25,624 --> 00:04:29,161
That is active transport, by
the sodium potassium pump.

100
00:04:29,811 --> 00:04:31,275
Now, the exam trap.

101
00:04:31,625 --> 00:04:35,555
Many students think every movement
across the membrane needs energy.

102
00:04:35,955 --> 00:04:38,287
But passive transport needs none.

103
00:04:38,287 --> 00:04:43,184
Diffusion and osmosis go from higher
to lower concentration on their own.

104
00:04:43,184 --> 00:04:45,410
Only active transport uses ATP.

105
00:04:46,060 --> 00:04:47,373
Where is this asked?

106
00:04:47,373 --> 00:04:51,215
As statement questions on the fluid
mosaic model, membrane proteins and

107
00:04:51,215 --> 00:04:52,431
types of transport.

108
00:04:53,031 --> 00:04:54,163
Exam level now.

109
00:04:54,163 --> 00:04:56,379
Read the assertion and the reason.

110
00:04:56,379 --> 00:04:57,558
Pause and solve.

111
00:05:02,958 --> 00:05:04,022
The answer is A.

112
00:05:04,022 --> 00:05:06,942
Both are true, and the reason
explains the assertion.

113
00:05:07,442 --> 00:05:08,670
The assertion is true.

114
00:05:08,670 --> 00:05:11,595
Polar molecules need carrier
proteins to cross the membrane.

115
00:05:12,095 --> 00:05:14,940
The reason is true too,
and it is the cause.

116
00:05:14,940 --> 00:05:19,084
The middle of the bilayer is made of
non polar tails, so polar molecules

117
00:05:19,084 --> 00:05:20,565
cannot pass through it.

118
00:05:21,165 --> 00:05:22,706
Here is your recap.

119
00:05:22,706 --> 00:05:24,290
Screenshot this card.

120
00:05:27,490 --> 00:05:29,676
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

121
00:05:30,076 --> 00:05:30,976
One.

122
00:05:30,613 --> 00:05:33,909
Who proposed the fluid
mosaic model, and when?

123
00:05:34,809 --> 00:05:35,709
Two.

124
00:05:35,182 --> 00:05:38,100
What is the difference between
integral and peripheral proteins?

125
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:39,900
Three.

126
00:05:39,516 --> 00:05:41,425
Which type of transport uses ATP?

127
00:05:42,525 --> 00:05:46,285
Next time: what keeps a plant cell
firm, and how does a cell pack and ship

128
00:05:46,285 --> 00:05:47,185
its proteins?

129
00:05:47,066 --> 00:05:49,946
We find out in the cell wall
and the endomembrane system.
